   "summary": "<p class=\"lmeta\"><a href=\"https://github.com/VerifiedQC/ForShor/blob/main/efficient_tables/fortran/phaseproduct_saturation_f90/src/sp_arithmetic.f90\"><code>src/sp_arithmetic.f90</code></a> · 204 lines · 9 procedures (internal ones included)</p>\n<p>The cost model, in saturating native integers. <code>ik</code> is <code>selected_int_kind(18)</code> and <code>INF</code> is <code>huge(0_ik)</code>; <code>sat_add</code> and <code>sat_mul</code> clamp at <code>INF</code>, so an overflow can only look like “too expensive”, and any result that needs a value past the native range is reported as an error rather than an answer.</p>\n<p><code>leaf_cost(multiplicity, width)</code> prices a <em>stop</em>: \\(R = \\text{multiplicity}\\cdot w^2\\) rotations at \\(4 + \\tau(R)\\) each. <code>tau_exact</code> is the integer rotation price: it brackets \\(\\lfloor \\log_2 (100R)^{615}\\rfloor\\) with interval arithmetic and falls back to a multi-limb evaluation (<code>tau_full</code>) when the bracket is ambiguous. <code>child_width(w, k, a, b)</code> is the width the recursion reaches under a table of arity \\(k\\) with profile \\((a,b)\\): \\(\\lfloor w/k\\rfloor + 1 + \\max(a,\\ (w \\bmod k) + b)\\), the scanner's rule.</p>\n<p>The module is copied from a separately preserved earlier implementation (its header says so). On the Lean side, <code>Planner/TauPrice.lean</code> proves that the rounded rotation price equals its exact integer form for every \\(R\\); that this Fortran function computes the same integer is checked by the tests, not by Lean.</p>\n<p><strong>Exercised by.</strong> Every driver compiles it. <code>tests/test_solver.py</code> and <code>test_surface_outer.py</code> recompute the leaf price and the child-width rule independently in Python.</p>",
