   "role": "the exact decision for one table",
   "summary": "<p class=\"lmeta\"><a href=\"https://github.com/VerifiedQC/ForShor/blob/main/efficient_tables/fortran/phaseproduct_saturation_f90/src/sp_oracle.f90\"><code>src/sp_oracle.f90</code></a> · 1060 lines · 25 procedures (internal ones included)</p>\n<p>The innermost exact question: is there a legal table of arity \\(k\\), with profile caps \\((a,b)\\) and total charge at most \\(C\\)? <code>cap_decide</code> answers it by searching the graph of normalized states (each register a primitive row, coefficients as signed base-32768 limbs whose capacity follows the cap), best-first with a binary heap, and <code>reconstruct</code>s a table when one exists. Tables are lists of five-integer records <code>(kind, dst, src, sign, shift)</code>; kinds 1–4 are add, shift left, shift right and negate.</p>\n<p>The same engine is exposed three ways: <code>cap_decide</code> with the \\(k=2\\) shortcut and pruning, <code>cap_decide_pruned</code> with dominance and heuristic switchable, and <code>cap_decide_generic</code> with neither, so tests can compare the optimized search with the plain state graph.</p>\n<p><code>cap_replay</code> and <code>replay_profile</code> are the independent <em>check</em>: they execute a literal table instruction by instruction, verify that it is legal and restores the registers, and return its charge and profile. The solver replays every table through <code>cap_replay</code> before it prints <code>STATUS OPTIMAL</code>. The header cites the prose proof this follows (<code>proof_notes/threshold/SATURATION_AND_SINGLE_EXP_FPT…</code>, §7).</p>\n<p><strong>Exercised by.</strong> <code>tests/test_local.py</code> (with <code>test_local.f90</code>), <code>test_threshold_positive.py</code>, and, through the threshold driver, <code>test_solver.py</code>.</p>",
